+**Parameters:**
+*   **Overlap Factor:** 15% (used to minimize visual artifacts near cell boundaries) [S1].
+*   **Centroid Height:** Fixed to a single value for the entire scene [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Boundary Artifacts:** Without an overlap factor, visual discontinuities may appear at cell boundaries. The sources specify a 15% overlap factor to mitigate this [S1].
-*   **Occlusion Pruning:** Pruning relies on a "coarse understanding" of geometry. Pruning too early may remove valid data; pruning too late reduces efficiency gains. The sources note a 2x further reduction in trainset size after this step [S1].
-*   **Altitude Variance:** The method assumes small altitude variance relative to horizontal differences. If the scene has significant vertical variation (e.g., multi-story buildings with distinct floors), fixing the centroid height may degrade performance [S1].
+*   **Boundary Artifacts:** Without the overlap factor, discontinuities may appear at cell boundaries. The 15% overlap mitigates this [S1].
+*   **Altitude Variance:** The method assumes small altitude variance. If the scene has significant vertical variation (e.g., multi-story buildings with distinct floors), fixing the centroid height may lead to poor spatial partitioning [S1].
+*   **Occlusion Pruning:** Pruning relies on a "coarse understanding" of geometry. If the coarse training is insufficient, pruning may incorrectly remove valid training data [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with the number of cells $|\mathcal{N}|$. Each cell stores its own MLP weights. This is significantly more memory-efficient than a single large MLP for very large scenes, as only relevant submodules are loaded/updated [S1].
-*   **Training Time:** Parallelizable across cells. The visibility partitioning reduces the trainset size per submodule by 10x compared to the aggregate trainset. Additional spatial data pruning provides a further 2x reduction [S1].
-*   **Inference Speed:** Fast lookup of the closest centroid ($O(|\mathcal{N}|)$ or $O(\log |\mathcal{N}|)$ with spatial indexing) followed by a single MLP evaluation.
-*   **Strengths:** Scalable to large outdoor scenes (e.g., city blocks). Handles lighting variations via appearance embeddings. Simple implementation via 2D grid tessellation [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed grid may waste memory in empty regions (though less so than dense grids). Less flexible than octree-based methods for highly non-uniform scene complexity. Assumes relatively flat terrain for centroid height fixation [S1].
+*   **Memory:** Scales linearly with the number of cells and the size of individual MLPs, but significantly lower than a single global MLP for large scenes because each submodule only learns local features [S1].
+*   **Training Time:** Parallelizable across cells. Visibility partitioning reduces the data volume per module by ~10x, and pruning adds another 2x reduction, accelerating convergence [S1].
+*   **Inference:** Requires finding the nearest centroid, which can be optimized with spatial data structures (e.g., KD-trees or grids) given the fixed tessellation [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Scalable to large-scale scenes (e.g., city blocks); handles lighting variations via appearance embeddings; simple centroid selection [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Fixed 2D grid may not adapt well to scenes with complex vertical structures; requires two-stage training (coarse then pruned) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Mega-NeRF was introduced in 2022 by [S1] to address the scalability issues of NeRF for large-scale virtual fly-throughs. It builds on the principles of NeRF [S1] and NeRF in the Wild [S1], extending them with spatial partitioning and appearance embeddings. The method was designed to handle scenes where a single NeRF model would be too large to train or render efficiently.
+Mega-NeRF was introduced in 2022 by the authors of the paper "Mega-NeRF: Scalable Construction of Large-Scale NeRFs for Virtual Fly-Throughs" [S1]. It addresses the limitations of canonical NeRF and early sparse methods (like SRF and KiloNeRF) when applied to very large, outdoor scenes. The method builds on the concept of spatial data parallelism, allowing independent optimization of scene regions. It also incorporates techniques from NeRF++ for foreground/background decomposition and NeRF in the Wild for appearance embeddings [S1].
 
